---
// The update of the privacy policy for Apassy Connect (implementation plan s7, W1
// and owner-needed step 3). A DRAFT: /privacy stays the policy in force. When
// Connect starts, the owner checks this text, the facts in lib/legal.ts are given,
// and its parts move into /privacy (the lead "There are no Apassy accounts" goes);
// then this page is removed. Keep it true to the code and to
// docs/security/trust-model.md and the decisions of implementation-plan-s7-connect.md.
import Legal from "../layouts/Legal.astro";
import OwnerFact from "../components/OwnerFact.astro";
import { REPO } from "../lib/site";
const UPDATED = "2026-11-03";
const doc = (path: string) => `${REPO}/blob/main/${path}`;
---
<Legal
  draft
  title="Privacy and Apassy Connect · apassy"
  description="What Apassy Connect, the hosted service, stores and why."
  eyebrow="Privacy"
  heading="Privacy and Apassy Connect"
  updated={UPDATED}
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Apassy needs no account. Apassy Connect, the optional paid service, has accounts. This text says what Connect
    stores, why, and for how long. When Connect starts, it becomes part of the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
  </Fragment>

  <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
  <p>
    The controller of the personal data of Apassy Connect is <OwnerFact name="legalName" what="legal name" />,
    <OwnerFact name="address" what="postal address" />. Write to <OwnerFact name="email" what="e-mail for privacy requests" />
    for any question or request about your data.
  </p>

  <h2>What changes in the privacy policy</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The first sentence becomes: "Apassy keeps your credentials on your devices. Apassy needs no account: only Apassy
      Connect, the optional paid service, has accounts. There are no analytics and no ads. We never receive your
      passwords, your keys, or the contents of your vault."
    </li>
    <li>
      The vault leaves your Mac only when you choose it: a backup file, sync through a folder that you pick, your own sync
      home, or the sync home of Apassy Connect. Each of them gets the encrypted vault file only.
    </li>
    <li>
      The iPhone and the Apple Watch reach your Mac on your local network, over your tailnet, or, with Connect, through
      its byte relay. With the relay, the connection stays encrypted with TLS between your devices, checked against your
      Mac's own certificate.
    </li>
    <li>
      Agents in the cloud can reach your Mac at its public name through the relay. That connection uses an ordinary
      public certificate for the name, as a Tailscale Funnel name does: whoever runs Connect, or controls the DNS of the
      name, could get a certificate for it and read those agents' requests. Your own devices do not depend on this.
    </li>
    <li>
      The bouncer brain runs on your Mac or on a server of yours. Only if you turn on the hosted brain of Connect does it
      receive the command and the task of each question (below).
    </li>
    <li>The sections about the browser extension and this website do not change.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What Apassy Connect stores</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Data</th><th scope="col">Why</th><th scope="col">How long</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>The account id, and the name, public key, and dates of each device of the account</td>
        <td>To sign your devices in and to show the list of devices</td>
        <td>Until you delete the account. A removed device is deleted at once.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Your e-mail address, from the checkout</td>
        <td>For the list of devices, for support, and for notices about your contract. No newsletters, no marketing.</td>
        <td>Until you delete the account</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The ids of your customer and subscription at Stripe, the plan, its state and end date, the number of people or seats</td>
        <td>To know which plan you have</td>
        <td>Until you delete the account. Invoices: as long as tax law requires.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The encrypted copy of each vault that you sync through Connect, its id, signed heads, hashes, sizes, and times</td>
        <td>The sync home of your vaults. Nobody can read a copy without your vault passphrase, which Connect never receives.</td>
        <td>Until you delete the copy or the account, and at the latest 90 days after your plan ends</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>In a Connect Family or Team: who is a member, the role, and the name of a person who asks to join</td>
        <td>So that the organizer or an admin can confirm and remove members</td>
        <td>Until the member leaves or the group ends</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The byte relay: counts of bytes and connections of your account</td>
        <td>For the limits of the plan. The relay carries TLS records, and stores none of them.</td>
        <td>13 months</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The hosted bouncer brain, only if you turn it on: the command and the task of each question to the brain, never a credential value</td>
        <td>To answer the question for you</td>
        <td><OwnerFact name="brainRetention" what="retention of brain questions and answers (ADR 0035)" /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Support: what you send to us</td>
        <td>To answer you</td>
        <td>Until the request is closed, then at most 12 months</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Connect keeps no audit of addresses: it does not store the IP addresses of your devices. The files of Connect hold no
    key that opens a vault. The <a href={doc("docs/security/trust-model.md")}>trust model</a> lists each of them. Connect
    runs on a server of <OwnerFact name="hosting" what="hosting provider and country" />, behind Cloudflare.
  </p>

  <h2>Payments by Stripe</h2>
  <p>
    Stripe processes the payments. At the checkout, Stripe collects your card or other payment method, your billing
    address, your e-mail address, and for Connect Team an optional VAT id. We never see your card number. Stripe Tax uses
    your address to compute the VAT. Stripe uses the data for the payment on our behalf, and also for its own legal
    duties and fraud prevention as a controller: see the <a href="https://stripe.com/privacy">privacy policy of Stripe</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Who else gets data</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stripe</strong> (Stripe Payments Europe, Ltd., Ireland): payments, invoices, and tax.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cloudflare</strong>: this site and the front of Connect. It keeps the usual request logs (IP address, time,
      address, browser) to run and protect the service.
    </li>
    <li><strong><OwnerFact name="hosting" what="hosting provider" /></strong>: the server of Connect.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Some of these providers can process data outside the European Economic Area. They do so under the standard
    contractual clauses of the European Commission or the EU–U.S. Data Privacy Framework. We do not sell data and do not
    use it for ads.
  </p>

  <h2>Legal bases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The contract (Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR): the account, the devices, the sync home, the byte relay, the plan, and support.</li>
    <li>A legal duty (Art. 6(1)(c) GDPR): the invoices and the tax records.</li>
    <li>Legitimate interests (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR): the security of the service and the protection against abuse.</li>
    <li>Your consent (Art. 6(1)(a) GDPR): the hosted bouncer brain. Turn it off in Apassy at any time.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      You can ask for access to your data, a copy of it, its correction or deletion, a limit on its use, and you can object
      to its use. Write to <OwnerFact name="email" what="e-mail" />.
    </li>
    <li>
      Delete your account in Apassy on your Mac (Settings › Connect): we cancel the plan at once and delete your vault
      copies and devices. Stripe keeps the invoices as the law requires.
    </li>
    <li>
      You can complain to a data protection authority: in Poland, the President of the Personal Data Protection Office
      (<a href="https://uodo.gov.pl">uodo.gov.pl</a>), or the authority of the country where you live or work.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Legal>
